hat session.\n \n The field then names the nearest packet whose `goal.md` still has an unticked completion criterion and a slice within the budget, trying the spec folder first, then its parent, and no packet further up, so the receiver starts on that goal without the operator typing it.\n@@ -115,11 +119,11 @@ Codex reads the goal nowhere, so no field is written. Codex keeps a goal per thr\n \n A spec folder that holds a `goal.md` does not show that the sender had a goal, so a Codex hand-over carries no goal packet. A Codex sender names its goal on the note's `Active goal:` line instead, because Codex still has no goal reader.\n \n-A written value is checked the way `spec_folder` is. The packet path must be repo-relative. Its directory and `goal.md` must stay inside the repository once links are followed. The packet passes when it meets these checks in this checkout or in the working directory the note names. A resolver that finds nothing, cannot prove the goal or cannot read its source leaves the field out. It never fails the hand-over.\n+A written value is checked the way `spec_folder` is. The packet path must be repo-relative. Its directory and `goal.md` must stay inside the repository once links are followed. The packet passes when it meets these checks in this checkout or in the working directory the note names. A note with a `goal_root` is checked in that worktree instead, and only while `git worktree list` still names it as a checkout of this repository. A `goal_root` that git no longer lists, or that a hand edit points anywhere else, carries no packet at all. Another checkout can hold an older copy of the same path, so the packet is never re-checked there. A resolver that finds nothing, cannot prove the goal or cannot read its source leaves the field out. It never fails the hand-over.\n \n The note carries the path and never the goal text. A copy would go stale while `goal.md` changes. When no packet is carried and the resolver has a reason, `prepare` and `keep` print one `[session-handover] goal not carried: <reason>` line on stderr. They leave the field out. A note without a `goal_packet` field claims and hands over unchanged.\n \n-`claim` prints the note body, then the `complete` command that closes the note. Some runtimes see the claim instructions only once. A model that answers straight from the body can skip that step. It ends its output with a `Goal packet: <path>` line when the packet still holds a `goal.md`. `consume` reports the packet as `goal_packet`. `skilled-loop` exports it as `SESSION_HANDOVER_GOAL_PACKET` to the relaunched session.\n+`claim` prints the note body, then the `complete` command that closes the note. Some runtimes see the claim instructions only once. A model that answers straight from the body can skip that step. It ends its output with a `Goal packet: <path>` line when the packet still holds a `goal.md`, and the line reads `Goal packet: <path> in <worktree>` for a packet with a `goal_root`. `consume` reports the packet as `goal_packet` and the worktree as `goal_root`. `skilled-loop` exports it as `SESSION_HANDOVER_GOAL_PACKET` to the relaunched session.\n \n The wrapper removes any value an outer session left from the first launch. It does the same for any hop whose note carries no packet. A first launch started by the printed manual claim command is the one first launch that gets a packet. The wrapper finds the claim seed among the operator's arguments by its prefix. It reads the note that seed names.\n \n@@ -137,7 +141,7 @@ The goal packet resolver and the note fields live in `lib/relaunch-core.cjs`.\n \n ## 4. GOAL IN A CLAUDE RECEIVER\n \n-Claude has no goal record a hook can bind. A Claude receiver therefore gets its goal as the start prompt, `/goal <slice>`. The slice is read from `goal.md` at launch with `goal.cjs packet <packet> --workspace <root>`. It is the file as it stands at launch and never a copy.\n+Claude has no goal record a hook can bind. A Claude receiver therefore gets its goal as the start prompt, `/goal <slice>`. The slice is read from `goal.md` at launch with `goal.cjs packet <packet> --workspace <root>`. It is the file as it stands at launch and never a copy. For a note with a `goal_root`, the root is that worktree and no other checkout is read. The receiver still starts in the sender's working directory, so the goal is set even though the packet is not under it.\n \n Only the `chat_slice` is sent, with no frontmatter, comments or anchors. The command must report `packet_budget=ok`. The slice must fit 4,000 characters, the limit Claude puts on a goal condition.\n \ndiff --git a/.skilled/hooks/session-handover/troubleshooting.md b/.skilled/hooks/session-handover/troubleshooting.md\nindex ddbdb9e38cb..d4fbf226576 100644\n--- a/.skilled/hooks/session-handover/troubleshooting.md\n+++ b/.skilled/hooks/session-handover/troubleshooting.md\n@@ -51,7 +51,7 @@ A corrupt relaunch JSON file makes `should-relaunch` decline with reason `no-fil\n \n ### The receiver shows no goal packet\n \n-A missing `Goal packet:` line or `goal_packet` field is not a refusal, and `prepare` still exits 0. The goal step never fails the hand-over. It prints `[session-handover] goal not carried: <reason>` on stderr instead. `prepare` and `keep` write it only when the sender's goal can be proven. A Codex sender, a Claude session whose log cannot be read and a session whose goal has ended all leave it out. A Claude session that never had a goal set also leaves it out. That happens when neither the note's spec folder nor its parent has an unfinished `goal.md` within the slice budget. A `goal_packet` the note carried is removed on the next call in the same cases.\n+A missing `Goal packet:` line or `goal_packet` field is not a refusal, and `prepare` still exits 0. The goal step never fails the hand-over. It prints `[session-handover] goal not carried: <reason>` on stderr instead. `prepare` and `keep` write it only when the sender's goal can be proven. A Codex sen